A Metal-Organic Framework with Tetrahedral Aluminate Sites as a Single-lon Li+ Solid Electrolyte
Microporous materials have been targeted as promising candidates as ion-conductive materials and as such focus, has recently turned to the evaluation of their performance as solid electrolytes for solid-state electrochemical energy storage devices.Of particular interest are lithium-ion batteries,which are currently the established power devices for portable applications and which may eventually be used to store power from intermittent renewable energy source. Improvement of commercial devices includes the development of new electrolytes, which play a critical role in a battery's performance. Most of the current technology relies on organic solvent-based liquid electrolytes: a mixture of organic carbonates with dissolved lithium salts. They typically suffer from stability issues due to detrimental side-reactions involving the mobile anions on the surface of the electrodes, resulting in decreased cell lifetime. The potential for Li dendrite formation and the flammable nature of the organic solvent can also cause safety concerns.[1l Two alternatives are currently proposed to circumvent those limitations: solid-state or mechanically robust electrolytes that prevent dendrites to cross the cell and create short circuits,and single-ion electrolytes where only the lithium cations are mobile while the anions are immobilized within a matrix to prevent sidereactions at the electrodes.The larger electrochemical window of solid electrolytes in comparison to carbonate electrolytes will also allow for the development implementation of high voltage cathode $(>4.8\lor)$ and low voltage anode $(<1.0\lor)$ materials.[2]
Several approaches have been investigated for the preparation of MOFs as efficient single-ion solid-state electrolytes. MOFs with unidirectional pore systems are of interest due to their potential to provide highly anisotropic lithium ion conduction paths if they can be crystallographically oriented. Strategies for MOFs involved functionalization of open metal sites, grafting of secondary building units (SBUs) and substitution of trivalent metal ions by a combination of divalent and monovalent metal-ions.[3, 4] Those strategies nevertheless systematically lead to substoichiometric incorporation of lithium ions. Recently stoichiometric loading of lithium ions was achieved by a single-crystal to single-crystal transition between neutral and anionic phases which lead to the highest Li+ loading in a MOF and as a consequence, a reasonably high ionic conductivity.[5]
Stoichiometric incorporation of negatively charged building blocks was recently developed for purely organic microporous polymer networks (MPNs) and covalent organic frameworks (COFs) by
