introducing tetracoordinate borate,[6, 7] tetra- and hexacoordinate phosphate,[8] and hexacoordinate silicon(9] ionic functionalities. Although only evaluated for the borate frameworks, this approach also proved to be promising for the synthesis of single-ion solid electrolytes.7/ In this context, aluminum, one of the most abundant metal on the earth's crust, is another interesting element as it is known to form negatively charged $A|O_{4}$ tetrahedral building units in natural aluminosilicate minerals of the feldspar and zeolite families. So far, the preparation of anionic microporous frameworks based on tetracoordinate Al sites has only been reported in synthetic zeolites and although aluminum has been successfully used as a metal source for the preparation of MOFs, no anionic MOF based on tetrahedrally coordinated aluminum SBUs has been achieved. Indeed, the chemistry of Al-MOFs is dominated by polynuclear SBUs composed of edge-or cornersharing ${\mathsf{A l O}}_{6}$ octahedra $.110,\ 11]$ Moreover, only two examples of MOFs, namely CAU-4 and CAU-20, constructed from isolated ${\mathsf{A l O}}_{6}$ octahedral units have been reported (Scheme 1).[12)
Herein,we demonstrate a simple strategy to prepare such anionic Al-MOFs based on tetrahedral ${\mathsf{A l O}}_{4}$ units allowing free Li+ loading values never before achieved for MOFs.
The synthesis of Al-Td-MOF-1 was adapted from a procedure to form molecular aluminate networks from 4-hydroxybenzonitrile and lithium aluminum hydride (LAH) $.^{\mathit{13}]}$ We hypothesized that a condensed amorphous aluminate framework would form by replacing the 4 equiv of 4-hydroxybenzonitrile with 2 equiv of 1,4- dihydroxybenzene, and that Al-Td-MOF-1 could be crystallized by further solvothermal treatment under basic conditions to ensure reversible Al-O bond formation (Figure 1). Thus, in a first step, 2 equiv of 1,4-dihydroxybenzene were treated with 1 equiv of LAH at room temperature in tetrahydrofuran to produce an extended aluminate framework in quantitative yield with the release of hydrogen gas. The dispersion was then directly transferred in a Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and further treated solvothermally  at $150^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 4 days in the presence of triethylamine as a base. This enhances the reversibility of the AlO bond formation and facilitates the recrystallization of the overall framework under the solvothermal conditions. Al-Td-MOF-1 was collected as a microcrystalline powder and activated at $170^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under high vacuum for 12 hours.
The structural integrity of the framework was initially assessed by Fourier transform infrared (FTiR) spectroscopy (Figure S1). The absence of O-H stretching vibrations $(3100-3600~\mathsf{c m}^{-1})$ in the FTIR spectrum of Al-Td-MOF-1 combined with the presence of an intense Al-O stretching band $(870\ c m^{-1})$ ） gave a first indication of the successful condensation of the reactants. Clear evidence of the structural integrity of the framework was given by solid-state NMR analyses.The $^{27}\mathsf{A l}$ CP-MAS NMR spectrum of Al-Td-MOF
1 indicated one unique resonating signal with second-order quadrupolar interaction (Figure 2).The pattern could be simulated with one single Al species with a quadrupolar coupling constant $(C_{\mathsf{Q}})$ of $4.71~\mathsf{M H z}$ ， an asymmetry parameter $(\eta_{\mathsf{Q}})$ of 0.1 and an isotropic chemical shift $(\delta_{\mathsf{C S}})$ value of $55.26~\mathsf{p p m},$ thus validating the presence of only one type of tetrahedrally coordinated Al crystallographic sites within the framework $.^{[14]}$ The $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ CP-MAS NMR spectrum indicated the presence of the two awaited signals that can be assigned to the two different carbon atoms of the 1,4- dihydroxybenzene building block (Figure S2). The presence of extra-framework lithium counterions was confirmed by the presence of one signal at -1.7 ppm in the $^\mathrm{7}\mathsf{L i}$ MAS NMR spectrum of Al-Td-MOF-1 (Figure S3). Finally, the amount of lithium, $2.50~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ， and the chemical composition of the framework were established by a combination of compositional and inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES) analyses (Section $\displaystyle{\mathsf{S}}2$ ,which were in good agreement with the formation of the targeted and fully activated Al-Td-MOF-1 constructed around tetrahedral aluminate nodes.
The low ligand substitution rates for Al complexes like those formed during the crystallization of Al-MOFs is typically known to hinder the formation of larger crystals suitable for characterization by single-crystal X-ray diffraction. As such, Al-MOFs are usually solved using powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD） data.{1115] No crystals suitable for single-crystal analyses could be collected for Al-Td-MOF-1, and SEM analysis indeed indicated the formation of small rectangular parallelepiped crystallites with sizes of $\sim100\:\mathsf{n m}$ (Figure S7). The crystallinity of Al-Td-MOF-1 was thus confirmed by PXRD analysis. The diffraction pattern of Al-TdMOF-1 could be indexed compatible with several primitive tetragonal unit cells with lattice constants of $a=11.3218\mathbb{A}$ and $c=5.3885\AA$ .According to the minimal transitivity principle,[16] a diamond net or intergrown diamond nets, respectively listed a dia and dia-cN (with $\pmb{N}$ defined as the number of interpenetrating net
