components) in the Reticular Chemistry Structure Resource (RCSR) $\boldsymbol{\underbar I}^{177}$ should form by linking tetrahedral $A l O_{4}$ sites with ditopic linkers. The degree of interpenetration of Al-Td-MOF-1 was initially evaluated by generating the dia-c4 net, since the parameters of the maximum symmetry-embedding of a dia-c4 net were almost met by one of the suggested indexing solutions (P4/nbm space group (No.125); $\Nu=2a/c=4.20).^{\mathit{11}\delta}$ This initial attempt led to an overall chemically unrealistic model characterized edges,i.e. distances between two Al nodes,that were too long (9.65 A). We therefore investigated the possibility of different degrees of interpenetration by further generating the dia-c2 and dia-c6 models. The dia-c2 model (final space group: $P4_{2}/n$ ,No. 86) gave not only a better match in terms of reflection intensities between the simulated and the experimentally observed pattern (Figure S8), but also chemically more realistic motifs in terms of bond lengths and angles (edge length $=8.45\mathsf{A}$ as a reference, see for instance the compound with CSD identifier FEXWEL) $\cdot^{\mathit{19}\mathit{1}}$ After geometry optimization applying the UFF force field[20] as implemented in the Forcite module of Materials Studic $\mathit{\Omega}_{\mathbf{\lambda}}[21\mathit{\mathbf{I}}]$ the additional extra-framework ${\mathsf{L i}}^{+}$ counterions were accommodated within the computational model at a chemically and stoichiometrically appropriate position (Wyckoff 2b） to compensate the anionic net charges of the framework. Good agreement was found in terms of reflection intensities between the simulated patterns of the refined final model and the experimental pattern (Figure 3c). The overall Al-Td-MOF-1 structure can thus be described as composed of one-dimensional chains of alternating edge-sharing $A|O_{4}$ and $\mathsf{L i O}_{4}$ tetrahedral sites describing unidirectional pore channels with a square window aperture of $\sim5\times5\mathbb{A}^{2}$ (Figure 3b). In terms of topology, the underlying net of Al-Td-MOF-1 is thus dia-c2 if the extraframework Li ions are not taken into account and if only the anionic framework is considered (Figure 3a). But as the origin of the large tetragonal distortion compared to the ideal cubic embedding of adia-c2 net can be attributed toan electrostatically-driven contraction effect to allow an effective charge compensation, the Li ions become topologically relevant. Thus, the topology of Al-Td-MOF-1 is best described as a uninodal 6-coordinated snp rod net in which the points of extension are defined by the oxygen atoms (Figure 3a).
The permanent porosity was evaluated by low-pressure nitrogen $(\mathsf{N}_{2})$ and argon (Ar) sorption studies on evacuated samples of AlTd-MOF-1 at $77\mathsf{K}$ and $87\mathsf{K}$ ，respectively (Figure S11 and S14). The ${\sf N}_{2}$ surface area $(S_{\mathsf{B E T}})$ was calculated to be $656~\mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ from the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET） model by selecting adsorption points satisfying the consistency criteria for application of the BET theory. The theoretical ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake was calculated geometrically with the RASPA code by "rolling" a nitrogen-sized
