spherical probe across the surface of the computed crystal structure, and was calculated to be $784\ m^{2}{\cdot}{\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ [2]  Thus, the measured surface area suggests that the synthesized Al-TdMOF-1 reached nearly its maximum theoretical uptake $84\%$ of the theoretical uptake). The pore diameter of Al-Td-MOF-1 derived by fitting a cylindrical pore non-local density functional theory (NLDFT) model to the collected Ar isotherm was $5.5\mathsf{A}$ and matched well with the proposed structural model (Figure S14). In line with previous reports on Al-based MOFs, Al-Td-MOF-1 showed high thermal stability as determined by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA). No sign of decomposition could be observed until $600^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Figure S16).
The ionic conductivity of Al-Td-MOF-1 was determined by impedance spectroscopy on cast thin films and compared to the aluminate framework collected before solvothermal treatment. Characterization of the latter aluminate framework by NMR spectroscopy (Section S4) confirmed that full condensation had already occurred, notably according to the presence of one single broad signal with a similar chemical shift as Al-Td-MOF-1 in the $^{27}\mathsf{A l}$ NMR spectrum (Figure S5). As expected the framework formed at that stage is amorphous and featured significantly lower surface area $(52~\mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ as indicated by PXRD and ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption analyses, respectively (Figure S10 and S15). The films were prepared by mixing the solid electrolytes with polyvinylidene fluoride in a 1:1 weight ratio in anhydrous N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone. The slurries were cast on aluminum foils and carefully dried to ensure complete removal of the solvent. The thin films of MOF and polyvinylidene fluoride were impregnated with propylene carbonate in a 1:1 weight ratio, sandwiched between two stainless steel electrodes and assembled into a pouch cell (Section S9). Room-temperature Nyquist plots (normalized by the ratio of area to thickness) along with an equivalent circuit model are shown in Figure 4a. Values of R1 were used to calculate the ionic conductivity and will include both bulk and grain boundary resistance. The plots indicated room-temperature Li﹢ conductivity values of $\upsigma=5.7\pm1.9\times10^{-5}$ and $0.7\pm0.2\times10^{-5}{\mathsf{S}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ for Al-TdMOF-1 and the amorphous aluminate framework respectively. Those values are in the same range than the best values reported for MOFs and borate COFs and MPNs.[3. 5. 7] lonic conductivity was found to scale with temperature (Figure 4b) and showed Arrhenius character (Figure 4c) indicating that a hopping mechanism is responsible for lithium transport whereby coordinated lithium diffuses to the nearest vacant site.[23] Low activation energies were extracted from Arrhenius plots,but show no dependence on degree of ordering of the material. These results indicate that the 8-fold increase in ionic conductivity may not result from a more open network created during crystalization rather from an increase in available charge carriers.We postulate that the amorphous aluminate framework is more likely to have dead-end pathways or defects that reduce the effective number of mobile charge carriers.
