The electrochemical stability of the Al-Td-MOF-1 in contact with Li metal was evaluated to demonstrate the suitability of the material as a solid electrolyte.Apouch cell was fabricated as was previously described with a lithium electrode substituted for a stainless-steel electrode. The resistance of the cell was monitored using impedance spectroscopy over the course of $72\mathsf{h}$ .Figure 4c shows the evolution of the cell impedance depicted as changes in the Nyquist plot. The initial $\mathord{\sim}500\Omega$ impedance of the cell rapidly decreases to ${\sim}30~\Omega$ within the first hour of cell fabrication.We hypothesize that the decomposition of propylene carbonate and the formation of a solid-electrolyte interphase at the lithium electrode may initially improve electrical contact at the interface. The lower resistance is maintained over the course of $72\ h$ as shown in Figure 4e.
In summary, we report a simple synthetic strategy for the synthesis of an Al-MOF constructed from anionic tetrahedral ${\mathsf{A l O}}_{4}$ SBUs. ${\mathsf{L}}{\mathsf{i}}^{+}$ conductivity competing with the best reported values in this class of materials makes this approach promising for ionic conductivity applications and for the elaboration of further singleion solid electrolytes. Notably, a significant increasein conductivity was observed between the amorphous aluminate pre-framework and the crystalline Al-Td-MOF-1 obtained after solvothermal treatment. Thus, showing that although the high Li+ loading in Al-Td-MOF-1 is crucial, the crystallinity of the materials also has a significant impact on increasing the number of mobile charge carriers that could otherwise end up partially trapped within a disordered analogue of the same materials with an equivalently high amount of extra-framework Li ions. Al-based MOFs have attracted great interest since their discovery due to the lightweight of aluminum and their high chemical and thermal stability. The work presented here now extends the range of potential applications for these unique microporous materials.
